<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anne Marie Louise d'Orléans, Duchess of Montpensier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1627–1693</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as La Grande Mademoiselle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anne Marie Louise d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, known as La Grande Mademoiselle, was a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and the richest heiress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fronde">Fronde</a> she sided with the rebels and in 1652 had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> of the Bastille fired on royal troops, for which she was exiled from court. To free her lover, the Duke of Lauzun, from prison she gave the Dombes and other lands to the king's son, the Duke of Maine, in 1681.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anne-marie-louise-dorleans-duchess-of-montpensier.jpg" alt="Anne Marie Louise d'Orléans, Duchess of Montpensier" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Gilbert de Sève / School of Pierre Mignard I · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anne_Marie_Louise_d%27Orl%C3%A9ans_-_Versailles_MV_3476_(cropped-edited).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
